  "body": "The [section of the HTML spec about rendering images](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#images-3) has the following text (emphasis added):\r\n> -> If the element is an img element that represents some text **and the user agent does not expect this to change**\r\n> The user agent is expected to treat the element as a non-replaced phrasing element whose content is the text [....]\r\n> \r\n> -> If the element is an img element that represents nothing **and the user agent does not expect this to change**\r\n>      The user agent is expected to treat the element as an empty inline element\r\n\r\nNote the bolded \"does not expect this to change\" requirements there.\r\n\r\nI don't think the spec ever says what to do, when the user agent DOES expect this to change. (E.g. if an image is loading slowly, and we don't have any data yet, but we expect that it's coming.)  So, that behavior is simply never defined in the spec right now, AFAIK.  It probably should be defined (or it should more be explicitly labeled as undefined).",
